INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_CIV','The Boundless City');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_CIV_PEDIA','There shall be no second city. For the first shall have no end.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The Boundless City began with a promise about who could be abandoned. Its founders placed the households of a flooded valley under one charter, then bound that charter to a single public hearth: The Heart of the World. A person could live beyond the hills, beside an orchard, or across a sea and still belong to the same municipality. Their distance from the first square would never, by itself, cancel their claim to water, justice or a place in the common accounts.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Across the centuries, that promise produced an extraordinary political landscape. Farms, harbors, factories, forests and island districts acquired the legal identity of neighborhoods. Visitors saw a country. Its inhabitants gave the country a street address. The enduring question was whether an administration large enough to reach the horizon could remain answerable to the person standing at its door.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]What the word city means[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]Boundless law defines a city through its charter, citizenship and final obligations. A continuous roofline is unnecessary. An orchard district may contain scattered farmhouses; a fishing ward may be separated from the Heart by weeks of water. The inhabitants share a municipal treasury, a court of final appeal and the right to seek correction in the same public register.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Outlying districts develop markets, workshops and meeting halls. They give themselves local names and sometimes look remarkably like independent towns. Their ward councils administer neighborhood affairs, while the authority to issue a separate founding charter, create a rival capital or discharge the common city''s debts remains unavailable to them.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]This arrangement makes the Boundless City a federation of neighborhoods inside one enduring municipal person. Local decisions can multiply as the territory grows. Ultimate responsibility remains shared.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The distinction matters most during hardship. A rich harbor cannot declare its poorer hinterland an inconvenient foreign country. The Heart cannot dismiss a remote ward''s flooded wells as the concern of another sovereign. The charter keeps both claims inside the same argument, however uncomfortable that argument becomes.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]The Council of Wards[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]The Council of Wards turns a vast territory into a place where competing claims can be heard together. Districts send representatives through arrangements that have changed over the centuries. Population supplies much of their strength, while minimum representation keeps a small island or thinly settled frontier from disappearing beneath the votes of the central streets.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The Council approves common taxation, major works and the distribution of the city''s limited resources. It also sets the terms under which the First Architect may direct the administration. Sessions are famous for arguments between wards that receive water and wards that surrender it, between ports that pay customs and inland districts that maintain the roads feeding them.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Distance makes representation expensive. Early delegates traveled with bundles of local testimony and remained at the Heart through a season of decisions. Later systems use rotating delegations, reliable posts and increasingly rapid communication. Important hearings still seek witnesses from the affected wards. An official cannot make a neighborhood present merely by pointing to it on a map.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Council procedure can be slow. Boundless political thought accepts some of that delay as the cost of keeping distant people inside the decision. Its harshest scandals arise when delay becomes a weapon and a ward must wait for a service its representatives have already proved it needs.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]How distant work is organized[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]Most people working a remote district live there. Orchard keepers tend orchards near their homes, quarry households occupy the quarry reach, and fishing crews return to their own ward quays. The city''s common workforce record coordinates their contribution without requiring a daily journey to the Heart.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]An assignment identifies a body of labor, an authorized task and the district in which the work occurs. Its record follows changes of season and occupation. A person assigned to a distant mine cannot simultaneously be counted among the workers staffing a central workshop. Good administration begins by refusing to spend the same person''s time twice.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The common authorities periodically establish priorities. A poor harvest favors provision; damaged bridges draw labor toward repair; an urgent undertaking requires particular crafts and materials. Ward officers translate those priorities into practicable assignments and report shortages the central tables cannot see.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Pending assignments are a familiar source of frustration. A district may be valuable while lacking trained workers, safe access or an available household willing to take the place. The city can preserve a request and prepare for it. It cannot create a livelihood by counting a vacant entry as an occupied home.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]One program of great works[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]The city contains many workshops and skilled communities. A foundry in one reach can supply castings, an island ward can prepare timber, and central specialists can finish components none of them could make alone. Their ordinary repairs and household trades continue while the common government pursues its larger plans.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Great civic undertakings draw on a shared pool of commitments. A new arsenal, university complex or monumental crossing requires appropriations, skilled labor and coordination across several wards. The administration cannot promise those same commitments in full to every competing project. It establishes an order of major works and revises that order when public need demands it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Advocates of concentration argue that the city can accomplish extraordinary things by accepting that discipline. Critics point to quays waiting for repairs while a fashionable central project absorbs the best artisans. Both understand the same limitation: a single civic purpose gives the city power only when it makes difficult choices about its resources.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The First Architect''s most consequential act is often an instruction to wait. A drawing can be beautiful, a district can be deserving, and the labor needed to fulfill both promises can still be unavailable.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_CLOSE','Close');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_CULTURE','Culture');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_DAWN','There shall be no second city. For the first shall have no end.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]First Architect, the seven crossings have placed their seals beside yours. Their people bring the memory of a flood, the tools of their trades and a question that stone alone cannot answer: who will keep the promises made between strangers?[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Raise the Heart of the World around that question. Give its wards a hearing, its Surveyors a road home and its Registry the courage to preserve an inconvenient truth. Let distant households share a common citizenship without surrendering the customs that make their neighborhoods their own.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The fields beyond the hills will need hands. The islands will need crossings. Every new boundary will enlarge the work already owed. Build patiently, defend those entrusted to you and leave room at the table for the person your finest drawing has overlooked.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The foundations are laid. How far can one city''s promise travel?');
