INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_LEADER','The First Architect');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_LEADER_PEDIA','The founding Architect is remembered as an engineer who understood that a structure could fail even when every stone remained sound. A bridge maintained by hostile neighbors, a granary unreachable through another settlement''s gate and a well whose users had no standing before its owner were all unfinished works. Their failure lay in the arrangements between people.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The ceremonial portrait shows the Architect holding a plan before a city that extends beyond its edges. Later generations understood that image as a promise of patient construction and a warning about the limits of any single person''s sight.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]Before the first signature[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]Accounts of the Architect''s early life disagree. Guild histories claim an apprentice of distinction; ward traditions remember a practical worker accustomed to taking instructions from people who would never use what they commissioned. The most consistent stories place them among the crews inspecting the Seven Crossings after the Great Inundation.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The young engineer discovered that separate reports could make a common disaster appear accidental. Each crossing had a convincing explanation for protecting its own embankment, keeping its own reserve and closing its own passage. Read together, those explanations described a system that had transferred danger toward the people least able to resist it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The Architect''s first surviving proposal contained very little ceremonial language. It specified an open flood channel, common measures, shared stores and responsibility for the bridge joining them. Its political radicalism emerged from that practical detail. Someone would have to answer for the whole undertaking, and every participating household would need a recognized way to demand that answer.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The charter''s later grandeur grew around this original method: examine the actual failure, identify the obligations connecting its parts and build an institution capable of keeping those obligations alive.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]The first servant of the works[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]The Architect became the first signatory to the new common undertaking and accepted responsibility for directing its construction. Their authority initially depended on the willingness of the crossing wards to supply materials, labor and witnesses. A plan that lost those people''s trust would lose the practical means to exist.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Later civic tradition emphasizes this dependence. The founder''s seal is usually drawn as an unfinished arch. Its opening admits another stone and leaves its bearer visibly responsible for work still owed. The robes of office carry measures and architectural motifs, while the public treasury records the materials used to make them.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The founder resisted demands to make their household a hereditary source of authority. The office of First Architect developed around stewardship of the common charter, with later appointments contested through the city''s changing institutions. ''First'' preserves the founding obligation. It gives no later holder a claim to have created the people they govern.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Public stories often improve the founder into an impossibly consistent example. More careful histories preserve the difficulty of their position. The same person needed to pursue a coherent program, accept criticism from its intended beneficiaries and admit that abandoning a favored drawing could be an achievement of government.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]A plan returned across the table[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]One often retold episode concerns a proposed monumental approach to the first bridge. The Architect''s drawing placed a broad avenue through crowded work courts, giving the new common square an impressive entrance. Residents arrived carrying a second plan that marked the wells, shared ovens, workshop doors and informal passages omitted from the official survey.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The accounts disagree about the meeting''s temper. In some, the Architect listens at once. In others, they defend the drawing for an entire afternoon before agreeing to walk the route with its opponents. Both traditions end with a revised approach, a shorter procession and the preservation of several courts the original plan would have removed.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The episode matters because its lesson can be applied against an Architect. A complete-looking drawing may contain a profound ignorance of ordinary life. The founder''s work becomes a standard of leadership when later residents use it to demand that their own evidence be heard.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The phrase associated with that hearing remains common in the wards: ''Return the plan across the table.'' It asks the planner to receive knowledge with the same seriousness with which they issue instructions.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]Defense of the common threshold[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]The founder''s political temperament favored preparation, durable works and the defense of people already within the undertaking. Defensive walls, secure crossings and reliable provision could discourage a war without requiring the occupation of another population''s capital.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]That preference did not make the early city harmless. Its growing demand for timber, stone and access created disputes with neighboring communities. Crossing agreements could be generous in language and unequal in practice. The Architect''s followers sometimes treated the benefits of municipal law as sufficient reason to bring others under it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Boundless chronicles preserve competing interpretations of the founder''s caution. Supporters praise a leader unwilling to spend lives on ornamental victories. Critics point to occasions when defensive language made the protection of a new claim sound like an ancient obligation. Later generations inherit both the protective ideal and the possibility of using it to excuse expansion.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The founder''s most enduring military principle places a limit on success: winning a battle does not provide the means to keep every promise that conquest would create. The city must decide which obligations it can actually sustain, including its obligations to people who would prefer to remain outside it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]An office larger than a life[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]The founding Architect was mortal. Their dates, original name and final resting place remain subjects of disagreement among the city''s traditions. Some wards claim a modest tomb; others maintain that the founder requested an unmarked place among the work crews. The shared public memorial is the First Stone and the obligations associated with it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Successive holders of the office inherit a name already burdened with expectation. Some act through representative councils and open accounts. Others narrow participation, concentrate authority or invoke emergencies to keep criticism away from their plans. The city repeatedly measures those governments against a founding story it continues to reinterpret.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Artists usually depict the original Architect rather than every later administrator. The image gives an office spanning many generations a human face, while unfinished plans acknowledge that the office''s duty outlives any bearer. Its familiarity should never be taken as evidence that one person has personally managed every district across every age.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The best memorial to the First Architect is an argument they could still lose: a resident able to place an overlooked fact before the city''s highest planner, and a government capable of changing what it intended to build.');
