INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_SURVEYOR','Frontier Surveyor');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_SURVEYOR_HELP','Replaces Worker. 3 Movement and 125% of the ordinary Worker base work rate. Completing an improvement on an owned plot more than five hexes from the capital grants 5 Culture once per plot per Boundless player. Repairs and routes do not qualify.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_SURVEYOR_PEDIA','The Frontier Surveyor carries the common city to places its central offices cannot yet describe with confidence. Their tools include a measuring chain, a plumb line, field books and the supplies needed to turn a proposal into a usable work. Their greatest practical skill is learning which questions a drawing has failed to ask.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]Measuring a place that already has a life[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]A survey begins with observation. Where does the river stand after heavy rain? Which path can carry a loaded cart? Who grazes animals on the apparent waste ground? What does a line drawn during the dry season mean when the channel floods?[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The Surveyor records measurements alongside testimony, distinguishing direct observation from a report still requiring confirmation. The distinction protects both the city and the people expected to live with its plans. An uncertain ford entered as a dependable crossing can endanger a convoy years after the original party has left.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Existing inhabitants are witnesses to their own place. Their knowledge can reveal a useful route, a burial ground, a seasonal harvest or an obligation absent from a distant office''s records. Careless officials sometimes mistake a community''s unfamiliar form of tenure for a lack of claims. Surveyors with influential sponsors have made that mistake profitable.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The profession''s better traditions require a record of objections. A plan can proceed despite disagreement only through the city''s lawful decisions, with the disagreement still visible in the file. A tidy drawing achieved by erasing its witnesses is considered a failure of the survey.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]The first useful work[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]Surveyors train in practical construction because a boundary alone provides little shelter. Their parties clear intakes, establish sound foundations, prepare landings and organize the improvements through which a district can support its residents. Specialized labor and local knowledge remain essential; an instrument case supplies neither food nor an unlimited crew.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The first completed work has a significance that later maintenance cannot repeat. It gives the city''s promise a form people can test. A reliable well, a workable terrace or an improved landing can turn a proposed district into a place where households choose to remain. Its completion report enters the common memory with the circumstances of the site and the people who helped make it useful.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Maintenance follows as a continuing obligation. A crossing inspected during one season must survive another. A damaged improvement requires repair, and the accounts should distinguish restoration from a new accomplishment. Celebrating the same work repeatedly while neglecting its condition is an old temptation of ambitious officials.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Surveyor songs preserve a severe instruction: ''Do not call a line a road until someone can come home by it.'' The words concern every undertaking whose apparent success on paper can conceal its failure in daily use.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE][COLOR_YELLOW]Return journeys and distant trust[ENDCOLOR][NEWLINE]A Surveyor''s work includes returning. Later visits test the assumptions of the first report and allow residents to identify effects the initial party could not foresee. A diverted stream may have weakened another holding. A successful landing may require a larger market. A route may have drawn traffic through a quiet court whose inhabitants were barely consulted.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Those reports can embarrass the Surveyor who first proposed the work. The profession''s oath places the duty to correct a record above the desire to preserve a reputation. The Registry keeps both accounts, so later crews can understand what changed and why.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The dangers remain ordinary and substantial: weather, isolation, illness, hostile forces and the failure of supplies. Traveling farther demands greater care and cooperation. The city honors the parties who bring back unwelcome evidence as well as those who return with promising plans.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The Frontier Surveyor''s emblem joins an instrument of measurement to the means of making a passage. It expresses the profession''s central bargain. The horizon offers room for another district, and every district brings people whose lives must count for more than the line around it.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_SURVEYOR_STRATEGY','Develop strong frontier tiles. Distant work still requires citizen reservations; territory alone produces no additional tile yields.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_TRAIT','The World Is One City');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_BC_TRAIT_HELP','One original capital, unlimited contiguous cultural reach, and real citizens working distant owned tiles. Survive capital loss while units remain. At 1 HP with an adjacent melee force, reclaim your original, demolish native-legal targets, or subjugate protected major settlements once. Subjugating every rival original capital can win Domination. See the guide for workforce and external-transfer limits.');
