INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_HEADING_3','What the word city means');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_HEADING_4','The Heart and the First Stone');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_HEADING_5','The One Charter');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_HEADING_6','The Council of Wards');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_HEADING_7','The First Architect and the College of Works');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_HEADING_8','The Common Bench and the right of petition');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_HEADING_9','The Grand Registry and the city''s memory');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_1','There shall be no second city. For the first shall have no end.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The Boundless City began with a promise about who could be abandoned. Its founders placed the households of a flooded valley under one charter, then bound that charter to a single public hearth: The Heart of the World. A person could live beyond the hills, beside an orchard, or across a sea and still belong to the same municipality. Their distance from the first square would never, by itself, cancel their claim to water, justice or a place in the common accounts.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Across the centuries, that promise produced an extraordinary political landscape. Farms, harbors, factories, forests and island districts acquired the legal identity of neighborhoods. Visitors saw a country. Its inhabitants gave the country a street address. The enduring question was whether an administration large enough to reach the horizon could remain answerable to the person standing at its door.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_10','A Boundless citizen carries an address within a common civic identity. Moving from the central workshops to a distant orchard changes the district responsible for immediate services. It preserves the person''s claims to lawful wages, appeal and recognition of their household.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The city''s early registers favored established householders and licensed trades. Migrants, tenants and seasonal workers often sustained the frontier while remaining poorly represented in its books. Later reforms broadened the forms of residence and testimony that could establish standing. Reformers insist that aid begin with a person''s need; the record can follow the meal, the rescue or the shelter.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Households vary. Some are families occupying one house. Others are shipboard communities, apprentices sharing a work court or laborers moving between seasonal assignments. Registry practice tries to keep obligations legible without forcing every life into the same domestic shape.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The right to move is one of the city''s most important safeguards. A ward may require an orderly transfer of a service or a lease. It may not turn an ordinary work assignment into hereditary confinement. Administrators continue to test that boundary through debt, permits and the selective loss of paperwork, giving the Common Bench a steady supply of cases.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_11','The city governs different kinds of distance through different scales of responsibility. Street committees handle matters that neighbors can inspect together: drains, shared courtyards, small repairs and the care of vulnerable households. Ward councils oversee markets, local reserves, elementary services and the people maintaining them.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]A reach is a group of wards linked by a practical corridor, such as a river basin, a road over a pass or a chain of ferry crossings. Reach boards coordinate services that exceed a single neighborhood. An upstream ward cannot sensibly manage a whole watershed alone; an island timetable must connect to the mainland quay it depends on.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Those boards exercise delegated authority. They share the city''s charter and remain answerable to its courts and common accounts. Their boundaries can change as routes improve or settlement patterns shift. They acquire no independent right to found another capital.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The resulting administration resembles a branching network. The Heart settles matters that must bind the whole. Reach offices coordinate services that must connect several districts. Ward officials handle the work whose facts are visible locally. The system functions when each level knows both the decisions it can make and the failures it must report upward.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_12','The frontier begins with people walking beyond familiar obligations. Surveyors identify routes, water, soils and existing uses. They speak with inhabitants and record the places where an official map disagrees with the land. A proposed district enters the common accounts through a declaration describing its bounds, its responsibilities and its relationship to the wards beside it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Land can be claimed before it is fully developed. The Registry distinguishes inhabited holdings, public reserves, seasonal uses and proposals awaiting labor. A colored margin on a map supplies no harvest. The city must still find people willing and able to work there and practical ways for them to live.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The expansion of a common civic culture helps make those arrangements possible. Shared measures, recognizable receipts and a familiar route of appeal reduce the uncertainty of joining the city. Frontier ceremonies celebrate that trust, though official celebrations often conceal disputes over consent and compensation.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Some of the city''s most damaging failures began when a promising district was entered too confidently. An unusable landing, a disputed water source or the removal of customary grazing could leave an impressive boundary around a place the administration barely understood. The better Surveyors treat every new line as an obligation to return and check what it has done.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_13','Most people working a remote district live there. Orchard keepers tend orchards near their homes, quarry households occupy the quarry reach, and fishing crews return to their own ward quays. The city''s common workforce record coordinates their contribution without requiring a daily journey to the Heart.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]An assignment identifies a body of labor, an authorized task and the district in which the work occurs. Its record follows changes of season and occupation. A person assigned to a distant mine cannot simultaneously be counted among the workers staffing a central workshop. Good administration begins by refusing to spend the same person''s time twice.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The common authorities periodically establish priorities. A poor harvest favors provision; damaged bridges draw labor toward repair; an urgent undertaking requires particular crafts and materials. Ward officers translate those priorities into practicable assignments and report shortages the central tables cannot see.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Pending assignments are a familiar source of frustration. A district may be valuable while lacking trained workers, safe access or an available household willing to take the place. The city can preserve a request and prepare for it. It cannot create a livelihood by counting a vacant entry as an occupied home.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_14','The city''s food system combines local production, dispersed reserves and exchange between districts. Most grain never needs to pass physically through the Heart. It moves toward the households, mills and public stores that need it. The common account records the movement so that a receiving ward and a sending ward can agree on what has been supplied.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Provisioners try to distribute risk. Grain kept near the people who will eat it is less vulnerable to a single lost bridge. Reserves at major crossings can answer a failed harvest elsewhere. Coastal districts exchange fish and imported staples for inland produce. The system needs labor, containers, maintenance, reliable measures and people willing to report a shortage before it becomes a disaster.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The charter forbids an official from counting the same reserve as available to several places at once. Periodic inspections compare receipts with what a storehouse actually contains. Mold, theft and optimistic estimates have defeated that rule often enough to make a storekeeper''s reputation a serious public matter.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Distance remains dangerous. Storms delay boats. Armies interrupt roads. A healthy district can be isolated while another struggles to feed it. The city''s response depends on the reserves and relationships it has already built. Its geographic ambition has never abolished hunger, and its moral standing is tested most sharply by whose hunger it notices first.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_15','Water made the original city possible and continues to expose its interdependence. Intake works, irrigation channels and drainage crosses ward boundaries. The College of Works therefore examines water in terms of whole catchments, while local crews inspect the wells and pipes their neighbors use every day.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Upstream rights carry downstream duties. A workshop''s permission to draw water does not excuse poisoning the next district''s intake. Waste removal, clean sources, burial grounds and quarantine arrangements appear in the same public arguments as monuments and harbors. Their advocates often struggle for attention because a repaired drain provides less spectacle than a new civic arch.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The city learns through calamity. Investigations trace sickness along a shared pump, a badly sited outfall or an overcrowded lodging court. Physicians compare accounts from several wards, and maintenance crews return to drawings that officials once considered finished. Later advances improve treatment and understanding, while adding expensive new systems that must themselves be maintained.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]No single office can inspect every ditch. Public health depends on trusted local reports, enforceable standards and a common authority willing to spend money on an uncelebrated neighborhood. The most practical expression of the city''s unity is often a ward accepting an upstream restriction because the people below it possess an equal claim to clean water.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_16','The earliest municipal network used towpaths, pack trails, ferries and walking messengers. Stone roads improved the busiest corridors. Later canals, railways and rapid communication changed the distances an official could traverse within a working life. At every stage, the city relied on a network appropriate to the tools it actually possessed.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Messages receive different treatment according to their purpose. Ordinary accounts travel in regular packets; urgent warnings go by the fastest available relay. Duplicate notices follow separate routes when a decision could endanger a district if lost. A receiving clerk marks the time and source, because a late message can be accurate about a situation that no longer exists.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Local authority makes delay survivable. A ward can open a reserve during an emergency, close an unsafe bridge or begin a rescue under standing rules. Its officers record the reasons and later answer for their use of that authority. Waiting for the Heart to authorize every ordinary action would make the city helpless.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sea districts extend the same logic across uncertain water. Their quays are civic thresholds, with recognized measures and duties on either shore. The ocean remains an obstacle requiring ships, skill and favorable conditions. A line joining two places in the Registry has never been a substitute for someone making the crossing.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_17','The common treasury receives revenues suited to the city''s changing economy: rents, customs, assessments, fees and contributions in goods or labor. The Council debates both the burden and the practical cost of collecting it. An assessment that consumes a poor district''s seed grain may produce a larger receipt and a smaller harvest.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Wards retain resources for agreed local responsibilities. Major appropriations enter the common budget so that wealthy corridors also support works whose beneficiaries cannot pay for them alone. Accounts identify who collects, who spends, what was authorized and which obligations remain unpaid. Independent inspection attempts to keep an office from becoming the private estate of its holder.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Trade with other peoples supplies goods and ideas the city cannot provide for itself. Boundless merchants use familiar municipal measures at home and learn foreign conventions abroad. Their contracts can cross a border without making the person on the other side a Boundless citizen.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The treasury''s deeper problem is political. Prosperous wards frequently believe they contribute more than they receive. Officials at the Heart can mistake their own preferences for common necessity. Public budgets force those claims into the open, but a legible account does not settle the question of what people owe one another. That question returns with every appropriation.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_18','The city contains many workshops and skilled communities. A foundry in one reach can supply castings, an island ward can prepare timber, and central specialists can finish components none of them could make alone. Their ordinary repairs and household trades continue while the common government pursues its larger plans.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Great civic undertakings draw on a shared pool of commitments. A new arsenal, university complex or monumental crossing requires appropriations, skilled labor and coordination across several wards. The administration cannot promise those same commitments in full to every competing project. It establishes an order of major works and revises that order when public need demands it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Advocates of concentration argue that the city can accomplish extraordinary things by accepting that discipline. Critics point to quays waiting for repairs while a fashionable central project absorbs the best artisans. Both understand the same limitation: a single civic purpose gives the city power only when it makes difficult choices about its resources.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The First Architect''s most consequential act is often an instruction to wait. A drawing can be beautiful, a district can be deserving, and the labor needed to fulfill both promises can still be unavailable.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_19','Most residents experience the Boundless City at the scale of a neighborhood. They know the stall that sells bread before dawn, the court where children practice their letters and the crew responsible for the drain outside their door. Their relationship with the wider city often arrives through a pay receipt, a repaired crossing or a cousin moving to another reach.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Housing ranges from dense central courts to farm compounds, floating lodgings and seasonal work camps. Ward rules address access, fire, shared walls and the use of public ways. Those rules can protect residents or be used to remove them for a grander plan. Compensation and the right to contest a removal remain recurring sources of litigation.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Markets give the municipality a common economic language. Measures are checked openly, prices circulate with travelers, and traders can appeal beyond a local official who protects a favored guild. Familiar law helps a distant resident conduct business without needing a personal patron in every district.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Daily life also preserves difference. A northern quarry ward may eat, speak and celebrate differently from a southern harbor. Joining the city adds common obligations to those customs. The most enduring forms of civic loyalty arise when that membership makes a person''s local life more secure, rather than requiring them to become a copy of someone at the Heart.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_2','Before the city, the river valley supported seven rival crossing settlements. Each maintained a ferry or bridge, a grain store and a barrier where travelers paid for passage. Their quays prospered in ordinary years. During the Great Inundation, their separate defenses worked against one another. An upstream embankment forced water toward a neighbor; a closed ferry stranded another settlement''s children; grain spoiled behind a toll gate while families downstream went hungry.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The First Architect entered the valley''s surviving accounts as the engineer assigned to examine the ruined crossings. Their report connected failures that each settlement had described as someone else''s misfortune. The valley already shared one river, one road system and one vulnerability. Its institutions had divided the responsibility for keeping those things alive.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The agreement that followed began with practical commitments: common weights, an open flood channel, a shared grain reserve and a bridge whose maintenance could not be withheld from a rival quay. The seven councils met around the foundation stone of that bridge. Their new charter made the crossings wards of one city and required a single public account of their obligations.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The famous prohibition on a second city emerged from this settlement. No new district would be made to purchase independence merely to obtain a hearing. The common government would have to grow until its responsibilities reached everyone whose labor sustained it.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_20','Ward schools teach the practical foundations of common life: reading a notice, understanding a measure, recognizing a contract and making a complaint. Apprenticeships transmit craft knowledge through families, guilds and public works crews. Specialized institutions at the Heart connect those local traditions to research and teaching that require unusual equipment or sustained support.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Knowledge moves in both directions. A frontier mason can identify a stone that central planners have never tested. Fishers carry observations of currents, and orchard keepers preserve the memory of dry years. Surveyors are expected to return those findings with their measurements. The Registry''s usefulness grows when it can record an uncertainty as carefully as a confident result.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Scholars speak of the city as a long experiment in cooperation. A method proven in one ward can be tried in another, compared and improved. Successful techniques can spread widely through common teaching and public standards.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The same machinery can preserve error. An approved method may acquire powerful defenders long after its limits are visible. The city''s great scientific disputes therefore resemble its political ones: whether an institution built to remember can admit that it has learned something requiring it to change.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_21','The charter does not give every ward the same gods. Temples, household shrines, philosophical schools and communities of devotion coexist with the civic institutions. Their members debate the moral meaning of an undertaking large enough to encompass strangers they will never meet.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Some traditions interpret the unfinished city as a sacred duty to extend shelter. Others warn that endless building can become an excuse for pride. Public arguments over burial grounds, holy water, rest days and the placement of roads require the government to address belief as part of lived community, rather than as an empty space on a drawing.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The city''s shared ceremonies concentrate on obligations people can witness together. At the Opening of the Roads, wards display repairs completed since the previous gathering and name works still owed. During the First Threshold festival, new residents hear the charter in their own neighborhood before receiving its common seal.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Architecture carries that civic language. Ivory stone catches the light around public entrances; deep blue marks the water and routes joining distant wards; gold appears on thresholds, measures and symbols of entrusted office. The most admired ornament identifies a useful thing and the people responsible for it. Splendor becomes suspect when it makes a building''s purpose harder to find.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_22','Frontier Surveyors serve where the city knows least and promises most. Their work combines measurement, construction and negotiation. A useful report identifies a possible road and records the people who already use the land, the season in which a ford becomes dangerous and the resources needed to maintain a new work.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]They travel in small parties with instruments, field books and practical supplies. A chain measures a distance; a plumb line checks a foundation; conversations establish facts no instrument can provide. The best Surveyors distinguish what they observed, what they were told and what remains uncertain.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Their first completed improvements carry particular civic importance. A cleared intake or a dependable landing makes a proposed district usable and gives residents a reason to trust its inclusion in the common city. The report that returns to the Heart records the work as well as the boundary.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Surveyors can also become agents of injustice. A hurried line may ignore a grazing pattern or give a powerful sponsor the appearance of lawful possession. Their oath therefore includes a duty of return: to revisit the consequences of a plan and record the evidence against it. The horizon may be another street waiting to be drawn, but it is already someone''s place in the world.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_23','Ward watches protect local stores, crossings and households. The Charter Guard provides forces able to respond across districts and conduct the common city''s wars. Garrisons depend on provision, cooperation and routes that an enemy can threaten. Defending an enormous territory requires choices about what can be held and which residents must be moved to safety.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The charter''s promise lends defense a strong moral vocabulary. It also gives ambitious commanders a dangerous language for demanding obedience. Emergency powers can centralize decisions quickly; their continuation after an emergency has passed is one of the Common Bench''s hardest recurring problems.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]In the charter ideal, the Council authorizes the common city''s wars and appropriates the means to conduct them. The Architect directs the administration supporting the Guard, while commanders answer for operations and the treatment of residents. Immediate defense can begin under standing emergency authority. Its use must later be explained in a public account, including the obligations incurred while normal deliberation was impossible.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Beyond the frontier, other peoples retain their own political lives. Agreements can recognize roads, exchanges, protected places and mutual obligations without folding every partner into the Boundless register. The city''s claim to unity at home supplies no simple answer to the question of another community''s consent.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Boundless military doctrine rejects the routine creation of occupied satellite capitals. Defeated powers may be compelled to recognize the Heart''s supremacy while keeping their own institutions. Those settlements leave deep resentments, and the words ''one city'' offer little comfort to people coerced by its army. Destructive campaigns are remembered as failures of civic ambition even when official monuments celebrate their victories. The charter''s noblest interpretation limits the city''s appetite as firmly as it binds its existing responsibilities.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_24','The Great Register Fire began in a store of old deeds beside the principal archive. Flame spread through shelving, counting rooms and offices whose occupants had assumed that a stone building made their paper safe. In the following weeks, claimants arrived with debts paid, boundaries agreed and wages due that the surviving central books could no longer prove.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The crisis exposed the danger of trusting a common memory to a single place. Powerful interests pressed for convenient new accounts. Some officials treated the loss of evidence as permission to abandon obligations. Ward copies, household receipts and the testimony of ordinary residents became essential to reconstructing the record.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The settlement established procedures associated with Registrar Ilen Vale: separated copies, recorded revisions, witnessed corrections and the duty to preserve a disputed entry while investigating it. No future archive could be called complete merely because the books at the Heart agreed with one another.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The fire changed the city''s understanding of unity. Its memory became more resilient when copies and witnesses remained distributed among the people it described. The principal archive continued to hold great authority, while its authority increasingly depended on the ability of a distant ward to produce evidence that corrected it.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_25','During a later dry season, low-quay wards watched water diverted toward a celebrated monumental avenue. Their petition carried ordinary drinking cups into the Council chamber, each bearing the name of a household whose supply had failed. Warden Teren Ash laid them along the route ministers used to enter the hall.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The administration initially answered with drawings showing the future benefits of the avenue. Petitioners answered with the work logs of closed wells and testimony from the crews sent away to maintain the new fountains. The Common Bench required the government to account for the immediate obligations its plan displaced.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The resulting order delayed the avenue and redirected labor toward the failing intakes. Its importance lay in who could compel that revision. A distant ward had forced the city''s highest planners to place an existing promise ahead of their preferred monument.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Later governments retold the episode as evidence of the charter''s wisdom. Residents preserved the less comfortable detail: the cups had to be carried to the chamber because the earlier reports were already in the Registry and had been ignored. An institution can remember a need perfectly and still fail the people experiencing it.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_26','The occupation of the Heart creates the city''s deepest constitutional wound. The first square, principal institutions and common seat of government fall under an enemy''s control. Surviving officers and communities can preserve copies, maintain loyalties and support forces in the field. Their authority has lost the place at which its public obligations are finally witnessed.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The charter''s defenders refuse to cure that loss by announcing a convenient second Heart. An emergency archive can keep records; a camp can shelter officials; a loyal ward can feed the Guard. Those actions preserve a claim to continuity and a duty to return. They do not rewrite the city''s foundation as though the people and promises left behind had become expendable.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Recovery consequently carries a meaning greater than the return of a palace. Public records must be reconciled, coercive acts investigated and households who lived through the occupation heard. Some residents will have cooperated to survive. Others will arrive with incompatible accounts of what happened to their homes.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]If no living force or community can sustain the city, its promise can fail. Boundless histories acknowledge cities lost to war, neglect and the destruction of trust. The possibility of an unfinished ending gives the duty of reclamation its urgency.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_27','The early city depends on boats, walking messengers and the pace of handwritten accounts. Its reach grows along water and passable corridors. Delegation is broad because news travels slowly. The great practical achievements of this age are durable crossings, dependable measures and officials who can be trusted to act before an answer returns.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Later administrations gain paper mills, improved instruments, better roads and larger technical institutions. Printed notices make rules easier to circulate. More frequent travel makes distant appointments and appeals less exceptional. The city accumulates enough knowledge to plan undertakings its founders could scarcely have described.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Industry gives that ambition a formidable new scale. Railways move food and materials, machinery increases productive capacity, and rapid communication brings the outer wards closer to common debate. It also concentrates wealth, produces crowded labor districts and gives the government new means to monitor those who challenge it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Every age inherits the same constitutional question in a different material form. Faster communication can make an official more accountable or make an arbitrary order reach more households. Better records can strengthen a citizen''s claim or deepen their dependence on a classification they cannot contest. The tools enlarge what the city can do. Its people must continue deciding what the city should be allowed to do.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_28','At dawn, an orchard household checks a water gate with the crew from the next holding. Their road milestone names a quay several days away and, beneath it, the Heart. A child attending the ward school recognizes the same seal on a lesson book and on the receipt for a repaired roof.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The market receives flour from another reach. The miller checks its measure against the ward standard, while a provision clerk records which store supplied it and which accounts must be settled. A courier leaves with copied petitions, corrected household entries and an engineer''s report about the bridge beyond the orchard.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The household''s eldest apprentice intends to move to a coastal workshop. That evening, a clerk prepares the transfer of residence and work. Their standing will travel with them. The house, the orchard and the people who remain behind keep their place in the same civic account.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]None of those residents expects the First Architect to inspect tomorrow''s water gate. They expect the city to have assigned someone who can, to have given that person the means to act and to provide a hearing if the duty is neglected. For most inhabitants, that expectation is the practical meaning of belonging to a metropolis too large to see.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_29','Boundless political life turns on an unresolved tension. Shared institutions can protect a distant person from local tyranny and permit a common authority to impose itself on every district at once. The Registry can remember an overlooked household and become a machinery of exclusion. A road can carry relief or an army.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The city''s answer is an enduring practice of correction: witnesses, divided authority, local responsibility, public accounts and people willing to bring evidence against a beautiful plan. These safeguards require work. Their names alone cannot protect the residents whose claims they are meant to serve.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The First Architect''s unfinished drawings have consequently become an ambiguous civic treasure. Admirers see room for every future neighborhood. Critics see the temptation to treat every horizon as a vacancy awaiting administration. Both return to the sentence written at the first threshold.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The promise of a city without an end survives through the duties its inhabitants accept toward one another. Each new district asks whether those duties can travel farther. Each neglected household asks whether they have traveled far enough already.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_3','Boundless law defines a city through its charter, citizenship and final obligations. A continuous roofline is unnecessary. An orchard district may contain scattered farmhouses; a fishing ward may be separated from the Heart by weeks of water. The inhabitants share a municipal treasury, a court of final appeal and the right to seek correction in the same public register.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Outlying districts develop markets, workshops and meeting halls. They give themselves local names and sometimes look remarkably like independent towns. Their ward councils administer neighborhood affairs, while the authority to issue a separate founding charter, create a rival capital or discharge the common city''s debts remains unavailable to them.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]This arrangement makes the Boundless City a federation of neighborhoods inside one enduring municipal person. Local decisions can multiply as the territory grows. Ultimate responsibility remains shared.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The distinction matters most during hardship. A rich harbor cannot declare its poorer hinterland an inconvenient foreign country. The Heart cannot dismiss a remote ward''s flooded wells as the concern of another sovereign. The charter keeps both claims inside the same argument, however uncomfortable that argument becomes.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_4','The Heart of the World grew around the first bridge foundation and the square where the crossings joined their accounts. The original stone remains in a low public court. Visitors often expect a throne. They find worn steps, a surveying mark and an inscription repaired by generations of stonecutters.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The square holds the common institutions: the Council''s chamber, the principal Registry, the high benches of appeal and the offices that commission works for the whole city. Its significance comes from the continuity of the founding obligation. A later palace can be grander, and a distant harbor can become wealthier, without acquiring the power to declare itself the original Heart.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Residents criticize the capital''s privileges constantly. Its ministers receive news first, its markets attract the best specialists, and its monuments can consume money needed elsewhere. The charter''s defenders answer those inequalities by demanding that the Heart perform its duties more faithfully.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]During the founding festival, ward representatives leave a clear passage across the square. Any citizen may walk through it carrying a petition. The ceremony gives the city a yearly test: whether the place that claims to stand for everyone can still make room for an ordinary person.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_5','The One Charter began as a compact for crossings and public stores. Later generations enlarged it into a body of law covering residence, inheritance, land use, work, taxation and the limits of emergency authority. Successive governments interpret those provisions differently. The city has known narrow merchant councils, reforming assemblies and periods of severe executive rule.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Three principles recur across those changes. A citizen''s standing follows them between districts. Public obligations survive the replacement of an official. A municipal promise must identify the means by which it will be fulfilled.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]That third principle explains the city''s attachment to engineering. A proclamation of clean water invites questions about the intake, the labor, the money and the ward responsible for repairs. A boundary proclamation invites questions about the people who will live there and the institutions they can actually reach.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The charter also limits the founders'' grand ambition. Membership requires an intelligible relationship to the common city. Existing communities have customs, property and memories that a survey line cannot erase. Whenever officials treat the charter as permission to take everything within sight, petitioners quote its oldest obligation back to them: the city must answer for what it claims.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_6','The Council of Wards turns a vast territory into a place where competing claims can be heard together. Districts send representatives through arrangements that have changed over the centuries. Population supplies much of their strength, while minimum representation keeps a small island or thinly settled frontier from disappearing beneath the votes of the central streets.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The Council approves common taxation, major works and the distribution of the city''s limited resources. It also sets the terms under which the First Architect may direct the administration. Sessions are famous for arguments between wards that receive water and wards that surrender it, between ports that pay customs and inland districts that maintain the roads feeding them.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Distance makes representation expensive. Early delegates traveled with bundles of local testimony and remained at the Heart through a season of decisions. Later systems use rotating delegations, reliable posts and increasingly rapid communication. Important hearings still seek witnesses from the affected wards. An official cannot make a neighborhood present merely by pointing to it on a map.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Council procedure can be slow. Boundless political thought accepts some of that delay as the cost of keeping distant people inside the decision. Its harshest scandals arise when delay becomes a weapon and a ward must wait for a service its representatives have already proved it needs.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_7','The First Architect directs the common administration and gives the city''s ambitions a coherent plan. The title recalls the founder''s place as the first signatory to the original undertaking. Later holders inherit its responsibilities; public art continues to portray the founding figure as the office''s measure and warning.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The College of Works supplies the expertise behind that leadership. Its engineers, physicians, provisioners, surveyors and experienced craftspeople assess proposals before the city commits its labor. A magnificent aqueduct must be checked against the water it can carry, the terrain beneath it and the maintenance its future users can afford.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The College advises and administers. The Council authorizes expenditure, and the Common Bench can hear claims that an undertaking violates the charter. The Registry records what was approved and what was actually delivered. Together those institutions place friction around the power of a talented planner.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Under the usual charter arrangement, the Council selects an Architect from publicly examined candidates. The College can attest to competence, while wards can produce evidence about a candidate''s previous works. Continued authority depends on the common appropriation and the terms of the appointment. Removal requires a public charge and hearing, so neither personal brilliance nor an unpopular decision alone becomes a substitute for lawful accountability.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]That friction has frequently failed. Some Architects cultivated the College into a closed order whose credentials excluded practical knowledge from the outer wards. Others mistook the clarity of their drawings for public agreement. The city''s best remembered leaders learned to send a plan back across the table when the people expected to live with it identified something the plan had missed.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_8','The Common Bench gives distant districts a shared route of appeal. Most disputes begin locally, before ward judges or traveling panels. Serious conflicts over charter rights, public obligations and the conduct of officials can reach the Heart. Copies of judgments return along the same routes as tax records and work orders.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Access depends on more than the existence of a courthouse. The city maintains petition clerks who can record a complaint in ordinary language, witnesses who attest to a household''s circumstances and traveling hearings for districts whose residents cannot afford a journey. The ideal of a single law has repeatedly required different practical ways to reach it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The celebrated Ardin judgment concerned a quarry household omitted during a register revision. Sen Ardin was told that a family missing from the current book could not claim compensation for an official demolition. The Bench ruled that the city''s mistake could not extinguish the people harmed by it. Testimony, older copies and the remains of the house established the claim.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The judgment became a standard answer to careless administration: a record is evidence entrusted to the city. Its defects create a duty to investigate. They do not give the city permission to pretend a person has ceased to exist.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Reforming charters provide long, staggered judicial appointments following ward nominations and Council confirmation. Removing a judge requires recorded proceedings for misconduct. Those arrangements try to keep an Architect from choosing the outcome of a case by dismissing its judge. Their success depends on the willingness to honor an adverse judgment and to keep courts accessible when the treasury finds their work inconvenient.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIV5_BC_TEXT_9','The Grand Registry links an immense territory through common descriptions. It records households, boundaries, obligations, appropriations, public assets and the decisions that alter them. A ferry, an orchard lease and a petition may concern the same family; compatible records let officials discover that relationship before imposing contradictory demands.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The Registry is distributed. Ward houses keep working copies, reach offices compare changes, and the principal archive preserves authoritative decisions and their histories. Duplicate books travel by different routes. Later technologies change how copies move and how quickly discrepancies can be found. They preserve the need to know who made a change, who witnessed it and which earlier entry it replaced.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ledgerkeepers do not gain judicial power by holding a pen. They must record lawful decisions, identify uncertainty and permit correction. Public accounts disclose what the city spends. Household information receives narrower access, because an administration able to locate every home can also expose every home to abuse.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The Registry''s most respected motto places both duties together: ''Publish the accounts. Protect the person.'' Keeping that promise becomes harder as the city grows. The struggle to make its memory useful without making its people transparent runs through the entire Boundless history.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_FACT_1','The unfinished arch on the seal of office signifies public work still owed. Later holders inherit the obligation attached to the first signature.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_FACT_2','The phrase "Return the plan across the table" asks a planner to receive the knowledge of residents with the seriousness given to an official drawing.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_FACT_3','Public portraits commemorate the mortal founder. The long history of the office belongs to successive generations, each answerable for the promises it chooses to keep.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_HEADING_1','The First Architect');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_HEADING_2','Before the first signature');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_HEADING_3','The first servant of the works');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_HEADING_4','A plan returned across the table');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_HEADING_5','Defense of the common threshold');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_HEADING_6','An office larger than a life');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_LIVED','Founding Age; precise dates disputed');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_NAME','The First Architect');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_SUBTITLE','Keeper of the First Promise');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_TEXT_1','The founding Architect is remembered as an engineer who understood that a structure could fail even when every stone remained sound. A bridge maintained by hostile neighbors, a granary unreachable through another settlement''s gate and a well whose users had no standing before its owner were all unfinished works. Their failure lay in the arrangements between people.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The ceremonial portrait shows the Architect holding a plan before a city that extends beyond its edges. Later generations understood that image as a promise of patient construction and a warning about the limits of any single person''s sight.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_TEXT_2','Accounts of the Architect''s early life disagree. Guild histories claim an apprentice of distinction; ward traditions remember a practical worker accustomed to taking instructions from people who would never use what they commissioned. The most consistent stories place them among the crews inspecting the Seven Crossings after the Great Inundation.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The young engineer discovered that separate reports could make a common disaster appear accidental. Each crossing had a convincing explanation for protecting its own embankment, keeping its own reserve and closing its own passage. Read together, those explanations described a system that had transferred danger toward the people least able to resist it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The Architect''s first surviving proposal contained very little ceremonial language. It specified an open flood channel, common measures, shared stores and responsibility for the bridge joining them. Its political radicalism emerged from that practical detail. Someone would have to answer for the whole undertaking, and every participating household would need a recognized way to demand that answer.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The charter''s later grandeur grew around this original method: examine the actual failure, identify the obligations connecting its parts and build an institution capable of keeping those obligations alive.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_TEXT_3','The Architect became the first signatory to the new common undertaking and accepted responsibility for directing its construction. Their authority initially depended on the willingness of the crossing wards to supply materials, labor and witnesses. A plan that lost those people''s trust would lose the practical means to exist.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Later civic tradition emphasizes this dependence. The founder''s seal is usually drawn as an unfinished arch. Its opening admits another stone and leaves its bearer visibly responsible for work still owed. The robes of office carry measures and architectural motifs, while the public treasury records the materials used to make them.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The founder resisted demands to make their household a hereditary source of authority. The office of First Architect developed around stewardship of the common charter, with later appointments contested through the city''s changing institutions. ''First'' preserves the founding obligation. It gives no later holder a claim to have created the people they govern.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Public stories often improve the founder into an impossibly consistent example. More careful histories preserve the difficulty of their position. The same person needed to pursue a coherent program, accept criticism from its intended beneficiaries and admit that abandoning a favored drawing could be an achievement of government.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_TEXT_4','One often retold episode concerns a proposed monumental approach to the first bridge. The Architect''s drawing placed a broad avenue through crowded work courts, giving the new common square an impressive entrance. Residents arrived carrying a second plan that marked the wells, shared ovens, workshop doors and informal passages omitted from the official survey.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The accounts disagree about the meeting''s temper. In some, the Architect listens at once. In others, they defend the drawing for an entire afternoon before agreeing to walk the route with its opponents. Both traditions end with a revised approach, a shorter procession and the preservation of several courts the original plan would have removed.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The episode matters because its lesson can be applied against an Architect. A complete-looking drawing may contain a profound ignorance of ordinary life. The founder''s work becomes a standard of leadership when later residents use it to demand that their own evidence be heard.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The phrase associated with that hearing remains common in the wards: ''Return the plan across the table.'' It asks the planner to receive knowledge with the same seriousness with which they issue instructions.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_TEXT_5','The founder''s political temperament favored preparation, durable works and the defense of people already within the undertaking. Defensive walls, secure crossings and reliable provision could discourage a war without requiring the occupation of another population''s capital.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]That preference did not make the early city harmless. Its growing demand for timber, stone and access created disputes with neighboring communities. Crossing agreements could be generous in language and unequal in practice. The Architect''s followers sometimes treated the benefits of municipal law as sufficient reason to bring others under it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Boundless chronicles preserve competing interpretations of the founder''s caution. Supporters praise a leader unwilling to spend lives on ornamental victories. Critics point to occasions when defensive language made the protection of a new claim sound like an ancient obligation. Later generations inherit both the protective ideal and the possibility of using it to excuse expansion.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The founder''s most enduring military principle places a limit on success: winning a battle does not provide the means to keep every promise that conquest would create. The city must decide which obligations it can actually sustain, including its obligations to people who would prefer to remain outside it.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_TEXT_6','The founding Architect was mortal. Their dates, original name and final resting place remain subjects of disagreement among the city''s traditions. Some wards claim a modest tomb; others maintain that the founder requested an unmarked place among the work crews. The shared public memorial is the First Stone and the obligations associated with it.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Successive holders of the office inherit a name already burdened with expectation. Some act through representative councils and open accounts. Others narrow participation, concentrate authority or invoke emergencies to keep criticism away from their plans. The city repeatedly measures those governments against a founding story it continues to reinterpret.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Artists usually depict the original Architect rather than every later administrator. The image gives an office spanning many generations a human face, while unfinished plans acknowledge that the office''s duty outlives any bearer. Its familiarity should never be taken as evidence that one person has personally managed every district across every age.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The best memorial to the First Architect is an argument they could still lose: a resident able to place an overlooked fact before the city''s highest planner, and a government capable of changing what it intended to build.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_TITLES_1','Steward of the One Charter');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO Language_en_US(Tag,Text) VALUES ('TXT_KEY_CIVILOPEDIA_LEADERS_BC_ARCHITECT_TITLES_2','First Servant of the Common Works');
